# [AUDIT](../wg/audit.html)

## Summary

The **AUDIT** (Agent Use of Delegation and Interaction Traceability Audit) Working Group forming Birds of a Feather (BoF) session was held at IETF 127. The session was chaired by Orie Steele and Yaroslav Rosomakho, with Christopher Inacio serving as the responsible Area Director. Thomas McCarthy-Howe volunteered as the session note-taker.

The session evaluated whether the IETF should form a working group to standardize interoperable protocols, data representations, and architecture for post-hoc verifiable auditing of multi-domain agentic interactions. The session included presentations on a reference architecture, three practical use cases, a review of the proposed draft charter, an open microphone discussion, and six formal session polls.

While there was strong consensus that the IETF is the appropriate venue for work in this domain, the session concluded that the current problem statement, scope, threat model, and privacy implications require significant refinement before a working group can be chartered.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### Architecture and Use Case Presentations

Presentations referenced the session slide deck [Audit BoF IETF 127](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-audit-audit-bof-ietf-127-00).

* **Architecture Overview**: Miriam Kühlewind presented an architectural overview for post-hoc auditing of non-deterministic, distributed agentic workflows operating across administrative boundaries. The scope distinguishes logging (event recording), traceability (event linking), auditing (verifiable proof for third parties), and observability (root-cause analysis). Standardizing mechanisms to collect, correlate, and cryptographically verify audit records across untrusted domains is the primary goal, while internal model training, real-time enforcement, and internal execution logic are out of scope.
* **Travel AI Agent Use Case**: Jonathan Rosenberg presented a multi-domain e-commerce scenario involving a user delegating a booking task to a travel agent (e.g., Expedia), which interacts with a airline agent (e.g., United Airlines) and internal reservation systems. The use case highlighted the necessity of cross-domain correlation IDs to trace delegation chains, pinpoint failure points (e.g., spending limits exceeded), and resolve post-transaction disputes.
* **Enterprise / Internal Operations Use Case**: Warren Kumari (presenting for Brian Trammell) described an intra-domain enterprise operational scenario (e.g., an agent misconfiguring data center resources). The presentation argued for auditing actions as close to the physical execution environment as possible and augmenting existing enterprise logging systems with delegative identity annotations ("on behalf of") rather than relying on internal model introspection.
* **Transactional / Purchasing Use Case**: Nick Steele presented a consumer transaction workflow (e.g., delegated grocery/water purchasing). The scenario emphasized auditing fulfillment states, payment settlements, and constraint adherence ("no substitutions") across user agents and merchant presence agents while minimizing sensitive payload disclosure.

### Charter & Scope Discussion

The open microphone session focused on whether the charter scope was appropriate, technically sound, and adequately protective of user privacy:

* **Privacy and Centralization Risks**: Mark Nottingham, Daniel Kahn Gillmor, and Ted Hardie raised severe concerns regarding privacy and market incentives. Parallels were drawn to web cookies and browser tracking, noting that creating verifiable audit mechanisms across domains risks enabling pervasive surveillance if mandatory logging is forced upon users. They argued that privacy preservation must be architected from the start rather than treated as an afterthought.
* **Stakeholders and Threat Model**: Eric Rescorla, Ben Schwartz, and Thibault Meunier noted that the charter lacked a defined threat model and failed to specify the relying parties/verifiers of the audit records. Ben Schwartz pointed out that the scope favored platform operators over users by excluding verification of model identity or model degradation ("nerfing"). Muhammad Usama Sardar highlighted lessons from draft-intra-handshake-fail, where incomplete threat modeling led to critical security vulnerabilities in transparency architectures.
* **Domain-Specific vs. General Auditing**: Several participants (Thorsten, Robert Carolina, Dan Petrie) noted that auditing frameworks in industry are tightly coupled to specific legal, regulatory (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), or domain-specific requirements (e.g., financial or contact center tracking like vCon). They cautioned against attempting a universal audit architecture for all agent interactions.
* **Existing Protocols and Standards**: Participants highlighted the need to build upon or profile existing standards rather than inventing redundant mechanisms. Specific references were made to OpenTelemetry (OTel), OCSF (Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework), Security Event Tokens (SET / RFC 8417), Shared Signals Framework (SSF), RATS (Remote Attestation Procedures), SCITT, and OAuth.
* **Venue and Urgency**: Proponents and supporters (Justin Richer, Pam Dingle, Steven Mee, Roberta Robert, Thomas McCarthy-Howe) argued that the agentic web is developing rapidly outside the IETF (e.g., in the Agentic AI Foundation / AAIF, ITU-T SG17/FG-TIDA, and vendor-specific protocols). They asserted that the IETF is the proper standards body to address cross-domain interoperability and privacy-preserving audit standards before proprietary implementations become entrenched.

